item+ function src/media.rs :: sniff_image :: pub fn sniff_image(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<ImageKind> { :: Identify an image payload by its magic bytes — never by a file name or a client-claimed type. SVG is deliberately absent: it is a scriptable document format, not a raster, and is never accepted or served by the image path, so there is no stored-XSS surface to make inert. :: direct :: api
item+ function src/mmap.rs :: MappedLattice::fetch_binary :: pub fn fetch_binary(&self, coord: &Coordinate) -> Vec<u8> { :: The payload a fetch of this scroll returns, mirroring `libphext::binary::fetch_binary` exactly: a well-formed binary scroll decodes to its payload; any other scroll (plain text, malformed binary, unoccupied) yields its own stored bytes verbatim — empty when the coordinate holds no scroll. Total: never fails, never panics. :: direct :: api
item+ function src/mmap.rs :: MappedLattice::read_scroll_bytes :: pub fn read_scroll_bytes(&self, coord: &Coordinate) -> Option<Vec<u8>> { :: The scroll's exact stored bytes: the overlay version if edited, otherwise the mmap span, uninterpreted. This is the raw layer the binary-scroll support is built on — a binary scroll's stored form is text (header + base64), but payloads are recovered from these bytes without any lossy conversion. :: direct :: api
item+ function src/mmap.rs :: MappedLattice::write_binary :: pub fn write_binary(&mut self, coord: Coordinate, payload: &[u8]) { :: Store `payload` at `coord` as a canonical binary scroll (header + base64 of the exact bytes), whatever the payload holds. :: direct :: api
item+ function src/mmap.rs :: MappedLattice::write_bytes :: pub fn write_bytes(&mut self, coord: Coordinate, payload: &[u8]) { :: Store `payload` at `coord` under the format's ingestion rule (libphext's `stored_form`, behind `insert_bytes`/`replace_bytes`): valid UTF-8 is stored as ordinary text; anything else becomes a binary scroll holding the exact bytes. Ingestion never rejects a payload. :: direct :: api
item~ function src/mmap.rs :: MappedLattice::write_scroll :: pub fn write_scroll(&mut self, coord: Coordinate, content: String) -> bool { :: Write content to a scroll at the given coordinate. If the coordinate already exists (in mmap or overlay), it's modified. If it's new, it's inserted. Binary-scroll guard: a well-formed binary scroll (an image, say) cannot be replaced through the text path — its stored form is a header plus base64, and a text edit would corrupt the payload silently. Returns false when the write is refused for that reason; the scroll is left exactly as it was. Writing the identical stored form back is a no-op success. Byte payloads go through `write_binary` / `write_bytes` instead. :: direct :: api
item+ function src/mmap.rs :: MappedLattice::write_scroll_raw :: pub fn write_scroll_raw(&mut self, coord: Coordinate, content: String) { :: Store a scroll's stored form verbatim, with no binary guard. This is the byte path: callers pass either canonical binary scrolls (from `libphext::binary::encode_scroll`) or stored forms they have already validated. Prefer `write_scroll` for text and `write_binary` / `write_bytes` for payloads. :: direct :: api
